  "articleBody": "When you're planning for wheelchair accessible doorways, it's easy to fall into some common traps that can make all your efforts less effective. First off, a frequent mistake is measuring the door slab size instead of the 'clear opening.' Remember, the door frame and stop significantly reduce the usable width, so always measure from the narrowest point when the door is open. Another oversight is ignoring thresholds; even a small bump can be a major hurdle for a wheelchair. People also often forget about the door hardware – if it's hard to grasp or turn, the doorway isn't truly accessible. Finally, overlooking the door swing and the maneuvering space around it can make an otherwise wide doorway still difficult to use. A door that swings into a tight hallway can be just as problematic as a narrow one. HH Remodeling LLC, serving the greater Denver area, can help you spot these issues early. We're experienced in making homes truly accessible and can guide you through the process, ensuring you avoid these pitfalls and create a space that works for you. Don't hesitate to reach out for a professional evaluation if you're unsure.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}